plan-of-record format and the J2-manifest-scope verification question.\ndocs/slice9-h3-1-blocked-fixtures.md:297:`kernel-trace-form` (`test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1839–1860`) injects `(def gate-execution-trace (stream/make 1000000))` once in setup and prepends a native `(stream/put! gate-execution-trace ['fn-sym arity])` side effect at the entry of each kernel arity body (native call — **not** a §8.10 guest call). Comparing completing runs with and without instrumentation yields exact integer step deltas:\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:62:| jolt Python parser limits surviving J5: no process crash isolation (an uncatchable native fault ends the jolt process); no worker-restart claim; no cancellation of an under-bound parse (time is bounded only through the input and nesting axes); TSan not run and LSan has no positive control; the FFI cleanup cannot recover when no pointer is obtainable or the release primitive itself fails; an above-bound source the JVM parses is refused on jolt (a recorded divergence, not a parity pin). J5b adds: the lookahead bounds (4,096 SLL links, 256 full-LL links past the outer context) refuse nesting the JVM parses (nested calls past 408 levels, bracket nests past 453 inside a call, unary runs past 253); time inside the bounds is not bounded by them: the pinned runtime parses a flat left-recursive chain in quadratic time (`1+1+...`: 16,000 operators 7.4 s, 100,000 about 340 s natively on macOS arm64; a 4 MiB chain hours) and an SLL decision whose lookahead walks a `*` chain grows faster (`1*1*...`: 2,000 operators 21 s, 4,000 about 490 s, refused at 4,097 links), so an under-bound source can hold the parse thread for minutes; the guards add up to about 2x on prediction-heavy shapes; a worker freed when no parse thread can be started is leaked, never destroyed on the caller's stack; the JVM oracle's own exporter overflows its default stack on a 20,000-deep tree (the comparison runs it with `-Xss1g`); a prediction whose context graph exceeds 4x the lookahead bound in distinct nodes refuses (a cap, not an undercount); measured, not gated: the backslash-joined chained assignment at 100,000 lines 89 to 150 s and the `1+` chain at 100,000 operators about 340 s (macOS arm64) | spec §7.6 | jolt seat | open; slice J5c (the work budget) implemented 2026-11-01 — the flat-chain and SLL-walk clauses above are now refused by the work axis (row 61); the other limits stand; precondition for J6 Python peers | P3 |\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:66:| Closed (J4a): jolt `RandomAccessFile.writeInt` was absent (`dao.jing.file` append-frame!), so the content log could not be written on jolt | J2 rerun 2; owner “both sets” 2026-10-23; J4a collab/1792851000000-engineer-j4a-file-io.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md | J4 (J4a) | closed: `dao.jing.file`'s eight file seams take `:jolt` arms into `dao.posix.file` (`src/jolt/dao/posix/file.jolt`, jolt-only descriptor plumbing outside the `dao.host` profile family, J4a review R1) over `native/jolt-file` (POSIX descriptors: pread/pwrite loops, fstat, ftruncate, fsync, one close; the s32BE prefix packed in C, review F2); `bb probe:jolt-file` all pass; the three exclusions (dao.space.stigmergy-test, yin.vm.macro-build-test, dao.jing.file-test) rejoined `test:jolt-core` and the 25 guarded bodies of the six suites restored; the fail-closed handle state after a torn append or failed fsync (review F3) remains J4c's | closed |\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:164:| jolt Python parser limits surviving J5: no process crash isolation (an uncatchable native fault ends the jolt process); no worker-restart claim; no cancellation of an under-bound parse (time is bounded only through the input and nesting axes); TSan not run and LSan has no positive control; the FFI cleanup cannot recover when no pointer is obtainable or the release primitive itself fails; an above-bound source the JVM parses is refused on jolt (a recorded divergence, not a parity pin). J5b adds: the lookahead bounds (4,096 SLL links, 256 full-LL 