e oracle site→C1 occurrence-key binding on the PRE-port shape of `yang/clojure.cljc` (outcome channel). The port has now converged yang.clojure's exception lowering onto the continuation encoding (the owner's directive; Phases 1-4, `8e1dab6d`→`35d09aaa`: continuation family in the clj prelude, consumer wiring, outcome machinery retired net −418 lines, coverage preservation restored, test updates). The port's design preserved the **intra-unit private tail lane** (deepseek's remedy: direct intra-unit calls stay direct tail application; only published/cross-unit entries take a bridge frame) — and that preserved tail lane is exactly the shape cc3's binding targets. The steward's depth measurements cleared the thresholds (155/155 overlap, max ratio 1.20×, max depth 28 frames; `test/yang/latte_oracle/depth_comparison.md`), so budget work is not at risk. This round proves the **binding** still holds.\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-of-record.md:60:| Trace/budget | `stream/make` 1000000; 2M-bound precedent | restated capacity + instruction bound for the added field |\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794405917000-engineer-cc3-fix-r8.prompt.md:6:# Task: cc3 fix round r8 — make failed retention observable (astra's F1) + the targeted regression (F4)\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794405917000-engineer-cc3-fix-r8.prompt.md:19:1. **F1 P1 — failed retention must be OBSERVABLE, not swallowed** (repl.cljc ~:1510): the r7 blanket catch restores program execution (correct) but produces an ordinary successful value with no provenance handle and NO diagnostic — and the indexer's Warning cannot report failures it never saw (index.cljc:549 consults only indexer state; note the indexer runs BEFORE retention, ~:2021, so a store can publish the index then refuse the provenance relation or record write ~:239; provenance-validation refusals are hidden the same way). **Required fix (astra's words, as spec)**: preserve program execution while retaining an admission-scoped failure diagnostic and making retention-only failures observable. Distinguish failed retention from an unclaimed admission. Concretely: catch around retention, keep the program running, but record the failure (which admission, which stage — relation write vs record write vs validation refusal) somewhere observable — e.g. the round's Warning surface (a second Warning line, or an extended Warning the index-test contract tolerates: read the existing test contracts first and extend them deliberately if needed, stating the contract change) or the session state `repl/call-provenance-handles` shape (a failure entry beside handles). No handle may be advertised for a failed retention; earlier completed handles survive; replay semantics unchanged.\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794403932000-engineer-cc3-fix-r7.muse-wonder-1.0.findings.md:7:**Mechanism (named).** cc3 r2 item 4 connected provenance retention to the production load path: `run-evaluator-stage` in `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc` wraps every loader with `retaining-load`, which — before calling the real `load-program` — calls `retain-packet-call-provenance!`, which calls `jing/materialize!` twice on the session's `:index-store`. Ordinary Clojure rounds do create a claim (verified by probe: evaluating `(defn g …)` then `(g 10)` populates `repl/call-provenance-handles`), so on a store whose `put-bytes-fn` throws, `materialize!` threw *inside the loader*, before the program ever ran. `run-evaluator-stage` propagated it, `eval-program`'s catch routed it to `consume-failed-round`, and the round rendered `Error: store refused the write` with the value (`3`, `5`) lost and the query path's `(:yin.repl.query/index-unavailable)` refusal never produced. At master there is no retention on this path: the program evaluates, and the publication failure is reported by the indexer's own stage as the trailing `Warning: this round's code is not in the published code index (materialize failed: …)`. The regression window is r2's wrapper (retention connection), made unconditional-on-claim in the r4–r6 rounds; cc3's diff does not touch `index.cljc`/`query.cljc`, confirming the wrapper as the sole suspect.\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794403932000-engineer-cc3-fix-r7.muse-wonder-1.0.findings.md:19:**Local run attempt.** `bb` is unavailable on this laptop (confirmed: no `bb` on PATH; node v26.1.0, clojure, java 17 present). The direct substitute — `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test --config-merge '{:ns-regexp \\\"dao.stream.ws-project-cross-node-test\\\"}'` — fails before compiling: \\\"SHADOW-CLJS FAILED TO LOAD … Syntax error macroexpanding at (shadow/cljs/util.clj:1:1)\\\" (no `node_modules` in this worktree; shadow 3.3.5 cannot load). Per the prompt's own fallback, the cross-node laptop run is **deferred to Shiva**, where the re-run of `bb test:sub:cljs dao.stream` must prove it. The CLJ standing net below was run with `-n dao.stream.ws-project-cross-node-test` as instructed; that namespace is CLJS-only, so the JVM runner silently ran the other four namespaces (124 tests) and did not exercise it — recorded here as unverified locally, not as green.\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794352090000-engineer-cc2-fix-r3.prompt.md:19:1. **F2 P0 — counter equality is not admission identity** (publish.cljc ~:582, `retained-call-site-rows`): the current match on root + `(:t program)` == origin batch number ignores the origin's source medium and member index, and the counters DIVERGE ORDINARILY: failed expansions advance the macro batch counter while only successes are staged (`yin/vm/macro.cljc` ~:1662, ~:1877), and ordinary name publication consumes an index transaction (`yin/repl/index.cljc` ~:513). Astra's collision: one failed expansion, then two admissions of identical root R — publication of B selects A's table. **Required fix**: carry an explicit indexed-program → full-admission-origin mapping from the point where the index and the expander actually agree (the admission record that stages each program), keyed by the FULL origin `[medium batch-token member-index]` + root — resolve `[admission-origin final-root]` exactly, never infer from counter equality. Where the mapping is absent, recovery is refused (claim-free publication, unchanged policy). Read the cited macro/index code first; if the expander's retained tables already key by full origin, thread THAT through instead of inventing a parallel structure.\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794352090000-engineer-cc2-fix-r3.prompt.md:21:3. **F5 — test gaps** (test/yang/clojure_callsite_test.cljc): add the cases the review names — (a) foreign table with same root and same batch number but different medium/member must NOT be accepted; (b) the drift collision (failed expansion between admissions) must publish claim-free, not borrow; (c) a deleted retained row must be REFUSED (unmapped/refusal entry), not reassigned to the publication origin; (d) `[]`/nil supplied tables publish claim-free. Reproduce each red first.\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794421000000-reviewer-cc3-r11-confirm-jupiter-r5.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:5:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/bash -lc 'pwd; git status --short; git rev-parse HEAD; cat docs/design/datom.world.md'\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc3-review\\\\n?? CC3-R11-BRIEF.md\\\\n897786cf298b34d2882d1ebc5579e021d0cac968\\\\n---\\\\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\\\\n\\\\n## Core Philosophy\\\\n\\\\nEverything is data.\\\\nInterpretation > abstraction.\\\\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\\\\nRestrictions are a feature.\\\\n\\\\n### Foundational Axioms\\\\n\\\\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\\\\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\\\\n   At system scale, a \\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\\\\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\\\\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\\\\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\\\\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\\\\n\\\\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\\\\n\\\\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\\\\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\\\\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\\\\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\\\\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\\\\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\\\\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\\\\n\\\\n## Tuples and Datoms\\\\n\\\\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\\\\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\\\\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\\\\n\\\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\\\\n\\\\n## Streams\\\\n\\\\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\\\\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\\\\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\\\\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\\\\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\\\\n\\\\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\\\\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\\\\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\\\\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\\\\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\\\\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\\\\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\\\\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\\\\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\\\\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\\\\nthe stream's own.\\\\n\\\\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\\\\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\\\\n\\\\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\\\\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\\\\n\\\\n### Host Boundaries\\\\n\\\\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\\\\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\\\\n\\\\n## Architectural Commitments\\\\n\\\\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\\\\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\\\\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\\\\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\\\\n\\\\n## Design Principles\\\\n\\\\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\\\\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\\\\n\\\\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\\\\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\\\\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\\\\n\\\\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\\\\n  ordinary streams.\\\\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\\\\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\\\\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\\\\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\\\\n  auto-index observer.\\\\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\\\\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\\\\n\\\\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\\\\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\\\\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\\\\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\\\\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\\\\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\\\\nretired `:global` tag in\\\\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\\\\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\\\\n\\\\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\\\\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\\\\n\\\\n## Code Style\\\\n\\\\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\\\\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\\\\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\\\\n- Avoid cleverness.\\\\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\\\\n\\\\n## Development Workflow\\\\n\\\\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\\\\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\\\\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\\\\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\\\\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\\\\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\\\\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\\\\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\\\\n  leaves to review.\\\\n\\\\n### Problem Decomposition\\\\n\\\\nIf a problem appears complex:\\\\n1. Decompose it into streams.\\\\n2. Identify invariants.\\\\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\\\\n4. Make causality explicit.\\\\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\\\\n\\\\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\\\\n\\\\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\\\\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\\\\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\\\\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\\\\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794421000000-reviewer-cc3-r11-confirm-jupiter-r5.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:7:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/bash -lc \\\\\\\"git diff --stat ced9f2c2..897786cf; rg --files collab/yang-clojure | rg '(1794403932000|1794405917000|1794407164000|1794416392000|1794418860000|1794412100000|1794408000000|1794416000000|1794417000000)'\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\" src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc                   | 394 ++++++++++++++-\\\\n src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc      |  95 +++-\\\\n src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc     |  92 +++-\\\\n src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc  |  87 +++-\\\\n test/d2_wedge_min_repro.clj              |  15 +-\\\\n test/d2_wedge_probe2.clj                 |  21 +-\\\\n test/yang/clojure_callsite_test.cljc     | 792 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++\\\\n test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj          | 790 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--\\\\n test/yin/repl/index_test.cljc            |  34 ++\\\\n test/yin/repl/query_test.cljc            |  31 ++\\\\n test/yin/vm/semantic/derive_test.cljc    |  50 ++\\\\n test/yin/vm/semantic/linearize_test.cljc |  76 +++\\\\n 12 files changed, 2386 insertions(+), 91 deletions(-)\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":1,\\\"status\\\":\\\"failed\\\"}}\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794421000000-reviewer-cc3-r11-confirm-jupiter-r5.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:9:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_3\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/bash -lc 'cat CC3-R11-BRIEF.md; rg --files collab/yang-clojure | tail -25; git diff ced9f2c2..897786cf -- src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc'\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"Created-GMT: 2026-11-12 05:5x GMT\\\\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-12 12:5x +0700\\\\nCoding-Agent: codex (jupiter POOL 2)\\\\nSession-ID: pending (provider-generated — fresh thread; the laptop astra thread 01a1c902 cannot resume from jupiter)\\\\n\\\\n# Task: Independent confirmation review — cc3 fix rounds r7-r11 (recovery trusts only shell-owned state)\\\\n\\\\nRole: Reviewer (adversarial, read-only)\\\\n\\\\nYou are confirming the final fix rounds of the cc3 arc (yang.clojure lowering provenance). The prior reviewer (astra on the laptop, thread 01a1c902) approved rounds r1-r6 and its r7-r10 confirms left narrowing residues; r11 implemented the structural fix that residue demanded. You review the LANDED STATE of those rounds with fresh eyes.\\\\n\\\\n## Where the code is\\\\n\\\\nBranch `cc3-lowering-provenance`, tip `897786cf` (pushed to origin). Two commits on top of master base `ced9f2c2`:\\\\n- `0ec036d1` — the cc3 feature (r1-r6, astra-APPROVED)\\\\n- `897786cf` — fix rounds r7-r11 (the shiva-attributed regressions + the forgeability class)\\\\n\\\\nReview the two-commit range: `git diff ced9f2c2..897786cf` (12 files, +2471/−176). You must clone or fetch the branch: `git fetch origin cc3-lowering-provenance` from any clone of git@github.com:sonwh98/datomworld.git — or work from the checked-out worktree if one exists on your host (check `~/ci/datomworld-*`).\\\\n\\\\n## Read first\\\\n\\\\n- The findings chain (all in collab/yang-clojure/ on the branch): `1794403932000-engineer-cc3-fix-r7...`, `1794405917000-engineer-cc3-fix-r8...`, `1794407164000-engineer-cc3-fix-r9...`, `1794416392000-engineer-cc3-fix-r10...`, `1794418860000-engineer-cc3-fix-r11...` (the last is glm-5.3-authored).\\\\n- The prior reviewer's confirmations: `1794412100000-reviewer-cc3-r7-confirm...`, `1794408000000-...r8...`, `1794416000000-...r9...`, `1794417000000-...r10...findings.md` — each left one narrowing residue; r11 was the structural response.\\\\n\\\\n## The r7-r11 arc's claims (verify each against the code)\\\\n\\\\n1. r7: retention materialization moved OUT of the loader's throw path (store-refusal no longer kills rounds; the round-text contract restored — `Error:` line gone, trailing `Warning:` preserved, query refusals produced).\\\\n2. r8: failed retention OBSERVABLE — stage-tagged admission-scoped diagnostics (`:validation`/`:relation`/`:record`), additive second Warning, session-state `:call-provenance-failures`; unclaimed ≠ failed; no handle for failed packets.\\\\n3. r9: shell-owned snapshots authoritative on the evaluator + link-raise paths.\\\\n4. r10: the render-hook wrap (unconditional session-state assoc, byte-identical passthrough for genuine failures).\\\\n5. r11 (THE STRUCTURAL FIX): retention evidence read EXCLUSIVELY from the shell's accumulator; the three exception-carriage keys REMOVED as dead code (grep-verified: no readers); the lazy-seq forgery test red-first 24F → 0F.\\\\n\\\\n## Adversarial focus (fresh eyes — find what ten passes missed)\\\\n\\\\n- Any remaining route from guest/host-controlled data into the retention diagnostics or handles (the carriage is gone — is the accumulator itself only written by shell code?).\\\\n- The accumulator lifetime: does `latest` (or its successor) survive/merge correctly across evaluator exceptions, link-resume, render throws, late-formatting throws, pending recheck?\\\\n- Duplicate-free guarantees on every merge path.\\\\n- Anything in the 12-file diff outside repl.cljc/tests that could carry retention data into canonical bytes or hashes.\\\\n\\\\n## Prior confirmation status (for continuity, not deference)\\\\n\\\\nastra r10 confirmed: the render-hook wrap, the ex-data passthrough, the php keyword coverage, the r10 tests. Its F2 residue (default formatter bypass) is what r11's structural fix terminates. Cross-node acceptance is CONDITIONAL on the shiva Node re-run (pending; the no-coupling analysis was ruled sound).\\\\n\\\\n## Output\\\\n\\\\nBegin the final response exactly with:\\\\nCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\\\\nCompleted-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\\\\n\\\\nThen per claim 1-5: `C<N>: CONFIRMED | OBJECTION: <...>` (severity + file:line for any objection), then `CONFIRMATION VERDICT: APPROVE | REQUEST CHANGES`. Static review only; no suites; no edits.\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793830000000-engineer-h3-1-blocked-fixture-diagnosis.agy-gemini-4-argon.stdout.log\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793309000000-engineer-adopt-fix1.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794160000000-engineer-wedge-encoder-layer.prompt.md\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793389000000-engineer-adopt-fix3.kimi-k3.1-pro.stdout.log\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.prompt.md\\\\ncollab/yang-clo