{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/exception-track/1794372000000-owner-decision-r3-vm-retained-seam.md | sed -n '1,130p';\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tCreated-GMT: 2026-11-11 01:30:00 GMT\n     2\tCreated-Local: 2026-11-11 08:30:00 +07 (+0700)\n     3\tCoding-Agent: interactive (ZCode / deepseek-flash, continuation-semantics orchestrator seat)\n     4\tOwner decision requested: ONE question. Nothing merges until you answer or until the shiva\n     5\tcontract numbers independently prove the same thing.\n     6\t\n     7\t# OWNER DECISION — may yang.clojure's fix add a VM-level retained-cell mark?\n     8\t\n     9\t## How we got here (one paragraph)\n    10\t\n    11\tClojure R3 (protocol-condition retention through the public escape) took four engineer rounds and\n    12\tthree independent confirmations. Each round's tests were green; each reviewer found a real defect\n    13\tthe tests could not see: a token transplant (R5), then a payload-identity regression that turned a\n    14\tRound-0 green cross-language row red, then content replay/mutation plus seven observation leaks.\n    15\tA three-architect mob was convened on whether a wrapper can ever be observationally identical to\n    16\tits payload: **2/3 ruled (A)** — deliver ordinary `:yang.call/thrown` payloads RAW (so the caught\n    17\tvalue IS the payload and every leak closes by construction), and keep the wrapper ONLY for\n    18\tqualified protocol conditions, bound to heap identity. muse-wonder (the dissenting (B) author)\n    19\timplemented (A) and returned **\"(A) DESIGN HOLDS\"**; glm-5.3 (author of the (A) rationale)\n    20\tconfirmed it independently: **CONFIRMED**.\n    21\t\n    22\t## What the fix does now (`fbd8da0f`, branch `exception-r3-impl`)\n    23\t\n    24\t1. `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc` — ordinary payloads raw; wrapper for protocol conditions only;\n    25\t   the per-call-site router and the witness closure DELETED. This half alone closes all seven of\n    26\t   the reviewer's observation leaks and restores the Round-0 round-trip row.\n    27\t2. `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc` + `module.cljc` (+82 lines) — an issuer-side `:retained` mark on a\n    28\t   heap entry, written only by a compiler-internal `:cell/new-retained` effect published as\n    29\t   `cell/dsg__new-retained` (a name guest source cannot spell); `:cell/set!` refuses marked cells;\n    30\t   a pure `cell/retained?` query beside `cell/authentic?`.\n    31\t\n    32\t## The decision\n    33\t\n    34\tglm's adjudication, verbatim in substance: *the seam is SAFE (521 tests / 6,188 assertions green\n    35\tacross the VM seam and all lanes; no invariant breakage), but AUTHORIZATION NEEDS-OWNER — the\n    36\tmob's (A) included this seam, while the track's written authorization named only the (B) fallback.*\n    37\t\n    38\t**Question for the owner: may the cell module publish the issuer-side retained mint/query, and may\n    39\t`:cell/set!` refuse marked cells — i.e. may R3 touch yin.vm at all?**\n    40\t\n    41\t- **YES** → clojure R3 merges as `fbd8da0f` (pending the shiva contract numbers), retention is\n    42\t  identity-bound and unforgeable by construction, and the `:retained` mark becomes a documented VM\n    43\t  seam with a `state.md` heap-shape correction.\n    44\t- **NO** → the raw-payload half still lands (it is separable: revert the two wrapper forms to the\n    45\t  witness versions). Consequence: protocol-condition retention keeps the witness-but-not-identity\n    46\t  binding, so the content-replay/mutation finding from round 3 stays OPEN and must either be\n    47\t  accepted as a known limitation (with the `:forged-retention` row documenting it) or deferred to\n    48\t  a later VM round.\n    49\t- **DEFER** → I hold `fbd8da0f` unmerged, land the raw-payload half alone, and the VM seam becomes\n    50\t  a separate proposal.\n    51\t\n    52\t## Evidence trail\n    53\t\n    54\t- Mob record: `1794369000000-mob-record-r3-retention-design.md` (2/3 (A), 1/3 (B), votes converge)\n    55\t- Design: `1794366000000-architect-mob-r3-retention-design.glm-5.3.findings.md`\n    56\t- Implementation verdict: `1794370000000-engineer-r3-glmsplit-implementation.muse-wonder-1.0.stdout.log`\n    57\t  (\"(A) DESIGN HOLDS\", 103 tests/993 assertions)\n    58\t- Independent confirmation: `1794371000000-reviewer-r3-A-design-confirm.glm-5.3.findings.md`\n    59\t  (CONFIRMED; seam SAFE; authorization NEEDS-OWNER; separable)\n    60\t- Foundational finding: `1794365000000-reviewer-r3-identity-confirm.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log`\n    61\t\n    62\t---\n    63\t\n    64\t## DECIDED: **NO** (owner, 2026-11-11 16:3x +07, in-session — verbatim: \"NO\")\n    65\t\n    66\tyin.vm stays untouched. The `:retained` heap mark, `cell/dsg__new-retained` mint, `cell/retained?`\n    67\tquery, and the `:cell/set!` refusal on marked entries are NOT authorized.\n    68\t\n    69\t### Consequences recorded\n    70\t\n    71\t1. R3 lands as the **raw-payload half only**: ordinary `:yang.call/thrown` payloads delivered raw\n    72\t   (closes all seven observation leaks; restores the round-trip row), protocol conditions retained\n    73\t   by the **witness mechanism** (c9c5533c's complete-contents closure), NOT heap identity.\n    74\t2. **Known limitation, documented here and in the ledger**: a guest that catches a protocol\n    75\t   wrapper can mutate its contents via `cell/set!` and substitute a different condition; the\n    76\t   boundary then forwards the substituted condition. Python/PHP do not have this hole (their\n    77\t   `ForeignException` is a native object with private state). This is a deliberate, owner-accepted\n    78\t   trade to keep yin.vm minimal.\n    79\t3. **R4's equivalence proof gains the decisive scenario**: does the outcome channel deliver\n    80\t   protocol-condition retention as securely as the continuation lane? The replay/mutation probes\n    81\t   (astra's battery) join the §8.10.9 scenario set. **Failure there triggers the owner's\n    82\t   pre-authorized (B) fallback** — under which the protocol carrier is expected to be a\n    83\t   closure-based immutable form (no VM change needed either).\n    84\t4. The rationale, owner's words in substance: adding VM lines to compensate for one frontend's\n    85\t   encoding inconsistency cuts against yin.vm's minimal-kernel design; the inconsistency is\n    86\t   handled at the layer that created it.\n    87\t\n    88\t---\n    89\t\n    90\t## FINAL DIRECTIVE (owner, 2026-11-11 17:0x +07, in-session) — SUPERSEDES the earlier reading of \"NO\"\n    91\t\n    92\tOwner, verbatim in substance across three messages: the \"NO\" vetoed **the divergence itself** —\n    93\t\"yang.clojure exception should converge. it should not be the exception.\" The VM seam was declined\n    94\tbecause kernel lines compensating for an encoding the owner does not want felt wrong — not because\n    95\tthe outcome encoding should be kept.\n    96\t\n    97\t**Decision of record: (B) TRIGGERS NOW.** yang.clojure's exception lowering converges onto the\n    98\tcontinuation encoding (the py/ts/php `%capture` raise-exit scheme). No VM change. R4 transforms\n    99\tfrom an equivalence proof into **port verification**; the earlier \"(A) now, (B) if R4 proof fails\"\n   100\tsequencing is superseded by this explicit directive.\n   101\t\n   102\t### The decisive argument (accepted by the owner through this exchange)\n   103\t\n   104\tReal Clojure — JVM, cljs, dart, jolt — uses dynamic unwinding with non-cooperating frames\n   105\t(throw-through-HOF works, `finally` runs from arbitrary depth), exactly the python/ts/php\n   106\tsemantics class; `recur` exists because those hosts lack TCO. yang.clojure's outcome model was a\n   107\tdivergence from **real Clojure itself**, forcing the restricted dialect (one catch, no finally,\n   108\topt-in outcomes). The port moves yang.clojure toward real Clojure semantics while yin.vm's native\n   109\tTCO + loop/recur (already compiling to true tail calls) preserves constant-depth iteration under\n   110\tany encoding.\n   111\t\n   112\t### Binding acceptance criteria (from the 2/2 depth mod, 1794396000000)\n   113\t\n   114\t1. LaTTe depth harness built FIRST; pre-port baselines recorded (the harness collects no depth today).\n   115\t2. The intra-unit private tail lane is PRESERVED in the port design (deepseek's remedy — no VM change).\n   116\t3. Uniform four-frontend probe: assert constant case only (`D(16)=D(64)=D(256)`, loop/recur +\n   117\t   handler-free chains); MEASURE the linear case at 8/32/128 × three categories; never require\n   118\t   growth contractually.\n   119\t4. Escalation thresholds to the owner BEFORE merge: any LaTTe workload >10× pre-port max depth or\n   120\t   >10,000 frames post-port.\n   121\t5. PHP's missing R1 bridge noted — \"every published entry\" becomes true for all four frontends\n   122\t   when the php round lands; the uniformity this directive asks for is then complete.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
