{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'cell|heap|mutable' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | head -55\"","aggregated_output":"21:5. **Code Identity (Content Addressing):** Because tempids (`:segment 123`) are local to a single machine's database, continuations must refer to code via immutable, versioned semantic profiles — a **canonical instruction vector** (positional tuples, not EAV) whose address is computed over the loader's resolved interpretation, whose addressed payload is the vector itself, and whose interpretation is pinned by a contract stamp versioning the *complete* execution contract.\n153: :yin.k/cells       {…}                        ; §7.5.3  cursor cells: logical identity + position\n164:`:yin.k/cells`, `:yin.k/store`, `:yin.k/scheduler`), *what world*\n235: :yin.k/cells         {cursor-cell {:yin.k/stream marker\n249:The spellings `:yin.k/cells`, `:yin.k/cell`, `:yin.k/response-cell` and `:yin.k/c-<n>` are the body-version 0/1/2 wire spellings, as the version-2 code (`ucf/handoff.cljc`, `ucf/remote.cljc`) uses them; version 3, the row carrier of `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`, spells the same cursor cells `:yin.k/cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/cursor-cell`, `:yin.k/response-cursor-cell` and `:yin.k/cc-<n>`.\n267:- The cursor cells the body carries are exactly the cursor cells its roots name:\n268:  none missing, none extra. A body with cursor cells claims\n831:left implicit on the emitter's heap:\n836: :yin.k/cell     :yin.k/c-17}          ; the cursor CELL id, §7.5.3 — not a bare position\n850: :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23         ; the response cursor cell, §7.5.3, at its KEPT position}\n859: :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23}        ; response cursor cell at its kept position\n862:(`:yin.k/cell`, `:yin.k/response-cell` and `:yin.k/c-<n>` are the version-2 wire spellings; version 3 spells them `:yin.k/cursor-cell`, `:yin.k/response-cursor-cell` and `:yin.k/cc-<n>`, 7.2.1.)\n873:  `:newest` skips past this call's own. The response cursor cell therefore travels\n874:  at its *kept* position, and the whole task's waiters on that cursor cell travel\n876:  waiters sharing a cursor cell consume successive values — are preserved on the\n880:  cursor cell through `dao.stream.remote.md`: the exporter enters the cursor cell's\n881:  handle in its table and the cursor cell's stream descriptor becomes a remote\n886:  assigning new positions. The cursor cell carries its kept cursor. The receiver\n905:  the carried response descriptor and the cursor cell's fresh local key (the cursor cell\n911:  descriptor in the frame, pending, and cursor cells; an attachment resolving to\n920:  its cursor cell. No wait is silently dropped and no\n926:`:cursor` (the kept cursor) is UCF's `:yin.k/cell`: the cursor-cell id stands for\n927:the cursor and its kept position rides in the cursor-cell table of section\n943: :yin.k/cell      :yin.k/c-31}            ; response cursor cell, minted newest\n951: :yin.k/cell      :yin.k/c-31}            ; the kept cursor cell, at its kept\n970:cursor cell; a `full` append keeps the request state, retains the envelope\n974:cursor at the first unconsumed position -- the per-cursor-cell round-order\n1049:  with its own frames, parked records, stores, cursor cells, code, and\n1112:- `:next`: `:yin.k/cell`.\n1113:- `:ffi`: `:yin.k/cell`, `:yin.k/call-id`; `:yin.k/op` optional. The\n1114:  response stream is the cursor cell's own stream marker.\n1118:  `:yin.k/response-cell`; `:yin.k/request-op` and\n1121:  `:yin.k/name`, `:yin.k/request`, `:yin.k/response`, `:yin.k/cell`;\n1153:| cursor reference | `:yin.k/cursor-ref` | `:yin.k/cell cell-id` | a store cursor entry for that cursor cell, §7.5.3 |\n1170:big integer inside a heap cell stays under the cell-lift refusal. Likewise\n1241:table, and lowers a logical cursor cell to a cursor entry in `:resources` seeded\n1243:entry -- two refs to one cursor cell still share one entry -- and no store key is\n1262:cursor cell in the receiver's `:resources`, issues a fresh reference sealed under\n1330:### 7.5.3 Sharing, cursor cells, and cycles\n1361:**Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref, cell.** The specification\n1367:| **cursor** | The immutable value of `dao.stream`: a logical-stream identity plus a position in that stream. It has no identity of its own; two equal cursors are the same value. `dao.stream.md`: \"A cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\" | `dao.stream` (`:dao.stream/cursor`) |\n1368:| **cursor cell** | The VM's mutable box `{:stream-id id :cursor c}` held in the task's `:resources`, which `stream/next!` overwrites with the successor cursor on every successful read. It has identity: two cursor cells holding equal cursors are two different cells. It is the identity-bearing object the row carrier serializes (the census, the numbering, aliasing). | `yin.vm.engine` `cursor-cell?` |\n1369:| **cursor-ref** | The sealed reference (`:cursor-ref`, sealed under the task's capability secret) that a guest program holds; it names a cursor cell. Binding one cursor-ref under two names makes both names the same cursor cell. | `yin.vm.engine` `issue-ref`, kind `:cursor-ref` |\n1370:| **cell** | Only the general mutable heap box, `yin.vm.cell` (`cell/new`), referred to by a `:cell-ref`. It is unrelated to streams. | `yin.vm.cell` |\n1373:cursor cell is plain data.* Together with the stream's portable descriptor it\n1375:and mints its own cursor cell, so the carrier never has to reconstruct the\n1376:sender's identity beyond naming which references share one cursor cell.\n1377:(2) *Sharing must survive transport.* Repeated cursor-refs to one cursor cell\n1378:advance together after transport, and two cursor cells at one position remain\n1379:two independently advanceable cells. Before 2026-10-12 the row carrier called\n1380:the cursor cell a \"cell\"; the version-2 handoff carrier keeps its shipped\n1381:spellings (`:yin.k/c-<n>`, `:yin.k/cell`, `:yin.k/cells`).\n1383:**Cursor cells.** A cursor cell is *observable state with aliasing*: two distinct\n1390:exactly this distinction: two cursor cells at one position would collapse, and\n1392:**logical cursor cells** separately from position content:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
