{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_59","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '1140,1178p';\"","aggregated_output":"  1140\tconstruction: **every map at an encoded-value position is a UCF marker**, and\n  1141\ta program's own maps are always wrapped.\n  1142\t\n  1143\t| Encoded form | Tag | Contents | Decodes to |\n  1144\t|---|---|---|---|\n  1145\t| scalars: nil, boolean, number, string, keyword, symbol | — | themselves (never a map; cannot forge a marker) | themselves |\n  1146\t| vector, list, set | — | same shape, elements recursively encoded (never a map) | same shape, elements decoded |\n  1147\t| literal map | `:yin.k/literal` | `:yin.k/entries [k₁ v₁ k₂ v₂ …]`, keys and values recursively encoded | the map, keys and values decoded |\n  1148\t| closure | `:yin.k/closure` | `:yin.k/segment addr :yin.k/entry pc :yin.k/params […] :yin.k/env {encoded}` | `{:type :closure …}` (§2.4) |\n  1149\t| reified continuation | `:yin.k/frame` | a nested frame (§7.4.1), recursively | `{:type :reified-continuation …}` |\n  1150\t| parked continuation | `:yin.k/frame` | a nested frame plus `:yin.k/parked-id` | `{:type :parked-continuation …}` |\n  1151\t| primitive | `:yin.k/primitive` | `:yin.k/name sym` | the named function, after profile check (§7.5.2) |\n  1152\t| stream reference | `:yin.k/stream` | `:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …` | an attached handle, under a fresh store key |\n  1153\t| cursor reference | `:yin.k/cursor-ref` | `:yin.k/cell cell-id` | a store cursor entry for that cursor cell, §7.5.3 |\n  1154\t| shared subvalue | `:yin.k/ref` | `:yin.k/id addr` | the table entry at `addr`, §7.5.3 |\n  1155\t| anything else | — | — | **refused**: `:yin.k/non-portable` |\n  1156\t\n  1157\tWhy the wrapping is total rather than an \"escaping rule\" for suspicious\n  1158\tshapes: a literal map is wrapped *wherever it occurs and whatever it\n  1159\tcontains* — including one that happens to read `{:yin.k/tag …}` — and the\n  1160\tentries inside a wrapper are themselves encoded, so a forged marker inside a\n  1161\tliteral is re-wrapped one level down and peeled back exactly one level on\n  1162\tdecode. No rule asks whether a map \"looks like\" data; no decoder decision\n  1163\tdepends on program content. Scalar and collection values pass through only\n  1164\tbecause no marker is ever a non-map, which the closed tag table fixes.\n  1165\t\n  1166\tThe scalar arm's \"number\" includes every exact-integer carrier Jing\n  1167\tsupports, not only what a host's `number?` admits: a JS or Dart `BigInt`\n  1168\tis a scalar like a `long`, encoded as itself and carried by Jing's major\n  1169\ttypes 0/1 and tags 2/3 (C3 converged ruling 4). No marker is added, and a\n  1170\tbig integer inside a heap cell stays under the cell-lift refusal. Likewise\n  1171\tJing's float64 carrier, how an integral float keeps its kind on JavaScript,\n  1172\tis a scalar number (float-address mob ruling); `yin.vm/plain-data?` and\n  1173\t`machine-data?` admit it for rows and machine payloads too, and rows,\n  1174\timages and hashing keep its bytes. Admission and round-trip preservation\n  1175\tof a numeric scalar do not imply that every primitive accepts it: on\n  1176\tJavaScript the standard arithmetic and ordering primitives meet the\n  1177\tcarrier's coercion refusal (`dao.jing.cbor.md`), while the JVM and Dart\n  1178\tcompute on their native double. That error is Jing's `:carrier-coercion`,\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
